// DISTRIBUTED textbook-scatter schedule for 19-histogram-unconstrained
// (TASK-0432 AC#2). The multi-worker broaden, identical in shape to
// 08-histogram/schedules/distributed.textbook.sched.nuc (TASK-0432 AC#1) —
// see that file's header for the full soundness reasoning. The ONLY
// difference is the FIXTURE: this example's `input.bin` is truly
// unconstrained (negatives and values >= BINS), so `bucket()` does real
// runtime work on each worker's i-band.
//
//   host    — owns I/O + the cross-worker partial-histogram element-wise-
//             sum combine.
//   w0..w3  — each owns a DISJOINT i-band of `input`, COMPUTES each
//             element's bin LOCALLY via `bucket(input[i])`, scatters into
//             its OWN private full-width partial histogram (whole-array
//             replicate: data-dependent write index => opaque dim =>
//             whole-array broadcast). Host element-wise-sums the partials
//             (TASK-0343 accumulate combine).
//
// Sound for the INPUT-INDEX partition (the iv `i` indexes the SOURCE
// `input[i]`, never the scatter target `histogram` affinely); a BIN
// partition stays FATAL via the halo `is_scatter_rmw` gate. The bin is
// computed LOCALLY per worker (no gather-index array crosses), so the
// TASK-0389 strict-FIFO Push/Wait trap does not bite. All `sync`
// transfers. Output bit-identical to `reference.bin` and to this example's
// single-worker `textbook` cell (verified, TASK-0432).

schedule for "../prog.textbook.algo.nuc" {
    workers = { host, w0, w1, w2, w3 };

    place load_input  on host;
    place save_output on host;
    place bucket on { w0, w1, w2, w3 };
    place inc    on { w0, w1, w2, w3 };

    loop i : partition=workers;

    transfer input     : sync;
    transfer histogram : sync;
}
